WEBVTT

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:02.910
Hello, glad to see you again.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.910
This being the last show of the series,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.430
we've decided to devote it to a question and answer period.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.800
We've had so many cards and letters from everyone who's watching us,

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.740
and today we're going to try to answer some of those questions right here on TV.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.750
Also today, it's a great, great privilege to me

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.170
to be able to introduce the pride and joy in my life.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.680
This is my son, Steve, and I've asked Steve to come give me a hand today,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.640
and he'll read questions for us.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.960
Steve, glad to have you with us.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.140
Good to see you today.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.290
Okay, I think everybody has probably asked

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.850
just about the same basic questions,

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.330
and we've picked a few letters here today

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.960
that will answer most of the basic questions.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.820
If we don't answer your specific question,

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.920
we will be dropping you a line personally

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.080
if it's something that only a few people ask.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.160
So, Steve, why don't you give us the first question?

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.580
Okay, the first question is, what is Magic White?

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:56.940
What is Magic White?

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.820
Magic White is a very thin...

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.140
White, oil-based paint.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.730
And it's very important that you know

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.110
that it's an oil-based paint.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.150
Please do not get it confused with gesso.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.520
Gesso is a water-based paint, it will not work.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.150
Magic White is designed to stay wet on the canvas

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.650
for extended periods of time.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.740
And maybe we'll just demonstrate here a little bit

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.960
how thin it really is.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.070
And let's see, if I can take just a little bit here,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.790
and we'll just put it right up here on the canvas.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.660
And it's very, very thin.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.200
almost like water, like so.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.890
And I hope that does give you an idea of what magic white is.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.050
The big thing is, do not confuse it with gesso.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.330
There is a terrific difference between the two.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.140
What's our next one, Steve?

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.040
How do I tell if I have too much magic white on my canvas?

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.190
It's a common mistake made.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.690
It's very, very easy to put too much magic white,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.270
and probably, well, let's just do it.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.980
Let me get an almighty brush here.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.740
And let's put some Magic White on the canvas,

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:03.880
and we'll just sort of demonstrate.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.450
And you'll put the Magic White on

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.980
and really work it back and forth.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.900
It takes a very thin coat of Magic White,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:21.810
just enough to get the canvas wet, just like so.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.590
If there's any doubt in your mind,

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.690
and you think you may have too much Magic White,

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.410
I suggest you clean your brush, dry it very well,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:29.960
go back and clean it.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.840
back over the canvas and remove any excess magic white

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.380
that's on your canvas, okay?

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.400
So you just rub this in very, very good here

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:44.800
and work it back and forth, there we go.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.540
That's even distribution of paint.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.790
Once again, if there's any doubt in your mind,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.700
clean your brush and go back over it.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.820
It will pick up the excess, okay?

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:01.190
What do we have next?

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.240
Can I use any kind of oil paint for this method?

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:06.950
The biggest thing that we're looking for

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.540
is a paint that is very, very firm.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.640
It has to be as thick as you can possibly get it.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.380
What we suggest is you go down,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.420
open the tube of paint, squeeze it.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.440
It should stand approximately three quarters of an inch

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.840
above the tube by itself.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.330
If, when you open it and squeeze it,

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:25.570
if the paint's sort of oily,

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.420
if it runs out the sides, it will not work.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:29.960
We suggest that you really

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.840
Let me try this, it has to be firm, okay?

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:34.430
What's the next one?

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.580
The brushes I purchased not seem to work.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.620
What do you suggest?

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.100
We run into this quite frequently.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.040
People will watch what we're doing here on TV,

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.630
and they go down to the local hardware store

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.790
and they purchase the cheapest brush they can find.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.800
Most of the time you'll end up with a nylon brush.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.960
The brushes that we use, let me just pick one up here.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:03:59.600
The brushes that we use,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.940
are natural bristle.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.860
These are split-bore bristle brushes.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.910
So when you purchase a brush,

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.700
get one that is natural bristle.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.800
Do not let them sell you a nylon brush.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.640
It will not work, it cuts paint.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.890
Okay, what's our next question?

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.650
Can I clean my brushes in turpentine?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.150
Turpentine, several things are wrong.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.620
Number one, it has a terrible odor

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.960
that will drive you out of the house.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.970
You'll be painting all by yourself very soon.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.600
soon. Probably the biggest thing that happens with turpentine, it leaves a residue in your brushes

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.720
that can only be cleaned out with soap and water. And if you use soap and water on natural

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.660
bristle brushes, you're going to find they get very fuzzy and begin to lose your shape. We suggest

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:52.100
that you clean only with odorless paint thinner and do not use turpentine. Okay, what do we have

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:59.970
next? Is it all right to use canvas boards to paint on? This is a question that's asked.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.060
over and over, because canvas boards are much more reasonably priced than stretch canvas.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.220
The big problem with using canvas boards is that inside the canvas board is a thin layer

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.140
of cardboard.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.220
The cardboard has a tendency to absorb your magic white, and your canvas will become

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.840
dry before the picture is finished.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:25.720
We really recommend that you use a canvas that's covered with an acrylic primer, double-primed,

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.180
two coats of acrylic.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.980
and we find that the stretch canvas is much better than the stretch canvas.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.120
much better than the canvas board.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:37.200
Also, the knife has a slight curve in the long edge.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.140
It is not straight.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:40.030
The curve is designed in there,

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.820
so when you push the canvas, it forms to the knife.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.720
So we really recommend that you use stretch canvas.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.060
What do we have next?

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.710
The snow on the mountains is so beautiful,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.480
but I'm having a hard time making the paint break.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.160
Can you tell me what I'm doing wrong?

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:58.640
Well, maybe we'll just demonstrate.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:05:59.960
And I think...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.700
I think I can probably answer most of the problems

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.860
you're having just by doing it.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.610
So let's just mix up a little blue-brown

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:08.470
and a little touch of alizarin and white,

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.740
and let's just build an almighty mountain.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:18.350
I think first of all, so we have a little background

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.080
here to play with, let's just put in a very quick

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.810
little simple sky and some blue, just so we have something

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.870
for the mountain to set here against,

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:29.880
rather than just a white canvas.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:42.870
We'll just throw in a little blue paint just enough to give us a nice little sky here

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:55.520
Several things are probably happening and we'll try to cover each one individually here

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.920
So let's make a basic mountain shape and let's just say

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.000
this is our mountain here.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.260
Probably the first thing that's happening

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:06.950
is that you're leaving too much paint on the canvas.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.410
We wanna remove all the excess paint that we possibly can.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.290
And we're pushing very, very hard.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:18.130
It's a matter of pushing the paint right into the fabric.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.230
So when you're doing this, get tough with it, get strong.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.370
This is the time to take out all your frustrations.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.340
It's much better than kicking the dog around the house

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:28.830
or taking it out on your spouse.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.760
This is the way you take out your frustrations.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:35.440
Now I've scraped all the paint off here that I can get.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.640
Now I'm gonna take the big brush and pull this down.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:43.320
This will take even more paint off and thin what's here.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.310
So there's virtually nothing left on the canvas

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:51.220
except just the color that's pushed into the material.

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:56.610
So here we have a basic mountain shape.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.770
If you're having problems with the snow breaking,

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.970
The most common problem is that you're pushing too hard.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:10.720
This is absolutely the most delicate touch imaginable.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.030
I remember when I was teaching Steve Dupain,

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.970
I used to tell him to pretend he was a whisper floating across a mountain.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:17.780
It's that light of a touch.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.080
So you touch it, just let it grab, and just the weight of the knife,

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:28.280
and you can make this snow just break and flow right down the mountain.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:29.980
But you need to...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.580
You can do this with, pretend your hand,

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.540
if you're not careful, is gonna float right away.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.040
It's that light of a touch.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:40.270
And then you can add all the snow that you want on here.

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:45.450
Think about where light's gonna strike.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.310
This is another problem that we run into.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.380
Think about your light, if it's coming from here,

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.870
where it's gonna strike all the little protrusions

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:59.190
that come up from the mountain, just like so.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.940
The shadows are just like the highlights,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:07.940
only we're going in the opposite direction.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:12.960
I think you've heard me say over and over in this show,

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:19.600
each highlight must have his own private shadow, each one.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.480
So if you want to make a peak, stand out,

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:27.810
give him his own private little highlight,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:29.070
or a shadow, I'm sorry.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:31.460
Okay, there we go.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.900
Probably the biggest problem, once again,

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:41.260
is too much pressure.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:44.710
Use a very, very delicate touch.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.050
And the diffusing is another area

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:49.330
we run into a lot of problems.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.800
And here, it's a light touch.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.720
A very, very light touch.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:56.970
It's so easy to destroy this.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:59.860
And especially if you're using a paint that's not super.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.060
Super, super strong, firm, you're gonna destroy all your work.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.170
So use a firm paint, barely touch the canvas,

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.220
just enough to diffuse this.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:18.400
And then we lift in the direction and angle

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.190
that the mountain is flowing.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:23.650
We lift it upward, just to take out the little tap marks.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:29.620
And just let it blend right into nothing.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:34.180
And you can just make that big old mountain

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:35.820
lay right around there in the mist.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.650
Okay, Steve, what's our next question?

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.270
Would you show us up close how to make the trees and bushes?

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.430
I'm also having trouble making the highlights

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:45.390
stick to the canvas.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.800
Another very common problem.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:49.940
Okay, let's make some,

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.100
I'll show you several different ways here

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:52.940
to make some trees.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:56.970
And maybe we can get the camera to come in real close

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.970
when we start this, and you can actually.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.420
You can actually see how the brush is bending.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.540
I'm gonna take a little blue and brown,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.630
a little touch of alizarin here, a little sap green.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.370
We'll just mix all these together.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.680
Now, if we can bring the camera in very close,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.860
you can see how full of paint the brush is here.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:17.200
It has a lot of paint in there.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.880
Okay, let's make a nice big leafy tree.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:24.190
We go into the canvas and we just bend the bristles.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:25.640
And you see what's happening.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:29.780
Thousands and thousands of little leaves.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:41.000
just happen and we can make this tree any size we want it just we just keep

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:48.190
working and playing on it here and it just grows right off your brush we'll

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:52.500
put another little one right here while we've got paint on the brush now this is

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:58.560
the way we make a leafy tree and one of the ways to make bushes for example is

00:11:58.560 --> 00:11:59.980
to use a big brush go straight

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.180
Take it into the canvas and bend upward.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:05.890
Just push upward, that's all there is to it.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:08.690
All we're doing is just bending the bristles,

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:10.600
make the bristles work for you.

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:12.390
You're the master.

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.320
Look at there, already we have a tree, mountains,

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:20.400
nice little bushes, ton of a gun.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:25.580
Okay, how to make an evergreen tree.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.080
We'll mix up some dark paint here,

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:29.970
and we'll just use the same color.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.760
blue-brown, little touch of alizarin, sap green.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:41.370
Very, very dark, and we'll take and fill a brush full of paint.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:45.790
Probably the biggest single problem we have

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.450
is not putting enough paint into the brush.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.680
It takes a lot of paint to stick these bristles together.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.800
Okay, let's just go right up here on the canvas

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.650
and let's make an evergreen tree.

00:12:59.390 --> 00:12:59.970
Let's just...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.360
All we're doing at first is just touching the canvas,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:04.800
just to sort of give us a guideline.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:06.790
Then we take the corner of the brush

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:09.210
and we start working from the center out.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:13.600
The center of the tree is normally always the thickest

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.920
because you have limbs on the back of the tree,

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:17.590
you have the trunk in the center,

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:21.260
and the limbs on the front, all of which stop light

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:24.200
and make the center of the tree look very, very thick.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.800
Okay, let me just finish this little tree up here.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.960
Another thing we worked very hard to

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.750
accomplish doing this, as tree limbs get older,

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:36.280
they have a tendency to hang more,

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:39.520
and underneath, the little needles and stuff begin,

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.050
oh, they get tired, some of them begin dying,

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:45.770
and you always have these little things underneath,

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:49.170
and for lack of a better word, I call them hangy downs.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.350
There may be a proper name for them,

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:54.070
but we just call them hangy downs.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:57.760
Okay, let's do that, let's do that again,

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.720
just so you can see one more time.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.040
And if we can get the camera in here real close,

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.070
let's do one right about here.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:08.380
We touch, give us a little guideline,

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:10.960
and we start right with the corner.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:12.840
So the first time it's just a little touch.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:15.270
Next time, a little bigger.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:17.720
And we're just working outward,

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:20.160
always from the center out.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:25.590
And nothing wrong with reloading your brush

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:26.730
several times through there.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.000
So you have a lot of paint.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.260
And we're not drawing with a brush,

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:33.960
we're just touching it and letting it come off.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.570
Yeah, so that's,

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:41.750
I'm gonna put some more little bushes right here.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:47.240
We also can use this one inch brush

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:48.660
to make all mighty trees.

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.830
And all you're doing is the same thing

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:52.690
we did with the big one.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:58.800
We just push, push, push, push.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.000
and you can use him sideways just like you did

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.020
the big brush to make all these nice little grassy areas

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:06.940
come down through here.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:16.860
And let's go back to the big brush

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:20.250
and just fill this up, give you an idea here.

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:29.020
Okay.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:33.350
The big brush covers a lot of space in a very short time.

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:35.050
Use it to your advantage.

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:41.230
Okay, I think the other part of the question was

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:44.400
they were having trouble making their highlights

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:45.340
stick on the trees.

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:48.980
So, let's put some highlights on these trees.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:53.270
First of all, I'm gonna take a little brown and white here

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:54.230
and make some trunks.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:56.900
Might as well show the trunks too.

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:58.470
All we're doing here is touching

00:15:58.470 --> 00:15:59.950
and pulling sort of to the left.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.730
And you can put as many of these little trunks in as you want.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:13.560
Here, just like so, and then just ever so often.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.620
And we can put little trunks growing everywhere.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:19.740
There they go.

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:24.440
Over here, we'll do the same thing.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:26.230
Just touch.

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:28.370
Indications, you don't see the whole trunk.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.460
because we're gonna have leaves in the front.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.450
Okay, now to put these highlights on,

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:43.990
we're gonna take and dip the brush into some magic white,

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.340
and then pull it through color.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.580
And maybe we'll make some nice green highlights.

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:53.920
I'll put a little touch of phthalo green in here.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:59.970
And all you do is just push, just enough to bend.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:08.760
the bristles and just lay all those beautiful little leaves on there. Thousands and thousands

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:16.120
of them. I used to spend weeks with my one-haired brush trying to do this. It almost made me angry

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:20.280
the first time I saw Alexander on TV that he could do in a matter of minutes what took

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:29.980
me days to do. Okay, there's nothing wrong with going back.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.300
back into your magic white and adding a little more.

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:37.210
So maybe we'll just put some highlights on here

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:39.940
and let you really see how the highlights are made.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:47.780
And if you want to put some little smaller bushes

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:51.070
down here, you're still using the same technique,

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:57.230
pushing with a brush just wherever you want.

00:17:59.350 --> 00:17:59.970
You can also...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.410
You can also use this brush sideways

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.570
and make beautiful little highlights.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:09.730
Just let them sort of wander around

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:10.570
wherever they want to go.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:13.450
You can spend a tremendous amount of time

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:15.530
worrying about where things should be.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:18.300
In the period of time that you spend worrying about it,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.480
you could have completed an almighty painting.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:22.740
For the evergreen trees,

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:26.460
bring the brush back to a nice sharp point,

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:27.880
just like I did to make the trees,

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:29.220
and

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.680
Start right up here at the top and just begin touching.

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:35.380
Evergreen trees are normally darker

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:37.680
than other trees anyway, so you don't want to kill

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.780
all this beautiful dark color that you've got in here.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:44.360
You start at the top on any kind of tree and work down.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:47.960
By working down, your brush is automatically picking up

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:49.530
the color that's underneath.

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:52.300
Automatically it gets darker as it goes down

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:56.670
because in nature, the light would come through here.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:59.140
It would strike the top of the tree normally first.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:01.600
That will be the brightest area.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.200
Also, as limbs get older,

00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:05.940
they lose a lot of their chlorophyll.

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:09.060
They begin losing a lot of their beautiful green color.

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:11.450
So automatically this happens.

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:12.730
You don't have to work at it.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:14.860
Let the brush work, let the paint work.

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:18.390
We'll highlight this other little evergreen over here.

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:21.170
Right here, okay.

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:23.510
We just take, just touch.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.440
And according to which side your light's coming from,

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:28.580
you want that side of the tree

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:29.900
to be just a little bit brighter.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:30.570
and the other one.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:33.800
Okay, while I'm over here,

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.960
I think I'll put some highlights on this other tree,

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:38.300
give you one more to look at.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:47.320
To the basic problem, if it doesn't stick,

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:52.140
majority of times, your problem is your paint is too thick.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.920
So add a little more Magic White with it.

00:19:56.880 --> 00:19:58.880
And we'll throw some little highlights on this one.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:04.740
Okay, let me wash out the brush.

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:14.450
And we'll put just a couple more little highlights in here

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:16.740
and see what our next question is.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:20.480
There we go, just let them fall in here,

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:24.760
wherever you want them.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:29.280
And another thing we do is mix the color on the brush.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:32.640
You've probably heard me say that throughout this series

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.100
several times, but it's very important

00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:37.260
because that way you get a variety of color

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:39.770
all on the same brush.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:44.940
Okay, I think that's enough play.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.180
And Steve, what's our next question?

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:49.280
I love the reflections you do.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:51.640
Would you go over the process you used to make them?

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.150
Hmm, that's one I should have had to ask a little earlier,

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:57.000
but don't think that's any problem.

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:59.050
Let me clean out my big brush here.

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:01.980
and we'll just see what we can do.

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:10.000
This is also a good example of how you can change a picture

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:11.160
right in the middle of the picture.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.660
So, Steve wants some reflection,

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:14.920
so let's give him reflections.

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:16.760
We'll just start right along in here

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:24.550
and start pulling this downward, just maybe like so.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:27.760
All we're doing is pulling the paint down.

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:37.440
Okay, now gently go across it,

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.310
just enough to give it a watery effect, don't destroy.

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:50.120
Now maybe you want to make a little more distinct reflection,

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:57.520
so let's put a nice little bright yellow bush right here,

00:21:58.830 --> 00:21:59.970
and let's reflect that wood.

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:03.170
right into the water, and we can just reflect

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:05.250
a lot of things while we've got it on the brush here.

00:22:10.540 --> 00:22:12.320
Maybe we'll put a nice little bush out here

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:13.100
that we can reflect.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.660
All I've done is turn the brush over,

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:18.140
and I'm putting the bushes right into the water.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.680
Okay, now maybe we can get a close-up shot right in here,

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:27.730
and let's see what we can do.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:29.970
We have the paint laid on the canvas.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.300
So now we're gonna take, and very lightly.

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:36.870
You almost let a hair touch, it's that light of a touch.

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:39.700
Gently pull that down.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:43.720
You don't wanna destroy, you just wanna diffuse it.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.420
Now we're gonna slightly, with a light, light touch,

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:49.010
just come across it to give it a watery effect.

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:59.980
Okay, now we can do the same thing over here, just like so.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.540
and you see what it looks like at this point.

00:23:03.110 --> 00:23:05.710
Don't worry about making it perfect with the first stroke.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:10.620
You have to pull down and then very gently come across.

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:12.760
And it's very easy to destroy this.

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:16.720
So all you want to do is just barely touch the canvas.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:19.970
Let a hair almost touch the canvas.

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:23.320
Another thing that we get a lot of questions about

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.440
is putting the water lines in.

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:27.420
So we might as well put some in.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:29.980
Here I'm gonna use just straight.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:34.100
Straight magic white, put a little bit on the knife,

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.670
and the one thing to remember here,

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:40.400
if you're making still water, it's always flat,

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:43.960
always level, so every little line that you put in it

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:45.520
must be basically flat.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:47.980
You can go anywhere you want to go with it,

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:50.830
but you have to keep them straight, okay?

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:52.950
Good example here.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:55.050
Looks like this is traveling right on back.

00:23:58.870 --> 00:23:59.970
Put a little more magic white.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.220
on our knife, and we're just cutting into the canvas.

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:11.600
Look there, and just let it go wherever you want it to go.

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.420
Okay, now maybe over here on this side,

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:19.360
we'll take a little bit of Van Dyke brown,

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:23.900
and we'll just show how you can make it look like soil,

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:27.190
dirt, rocks, whatever, and we're just laying

00:24:27.190 --> 00:24:29.850
a little color on here, straight.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.070
Van Dyke brown, okay, then we'll take a little blue,

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:40.330
white, and brown, just like we were making mountains again.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:43.980
Let it break, let it break, leave these open spots

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:52.550
so it gives the appearance of dirt or stones or, okay.

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:55.370
We can put a little water line underneath here

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:56.230
with the magic white.

00:24:56.650 --> 00:24:58.910
You're still keeping these lines basically straight.

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:19.050
Okay, we'll just add a few little sticks here and there.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.610
Maybe you wanna show a little something happening here.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:29.580
You can just pull that down a little.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:32.360
so it's not just a straight line going across.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:37.930
Just to break it up a little bit.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:40.680
And we'll put some little sticks here and there.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:49.630
And maybe we'll take just a touch more color

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:51.750
and let it run down this dirt area here

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.670
so it looks like it all comes together.

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:56.260
You don't want to have a straight line right across there.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:25:59.970
So we'll just break it up here and there with some little.

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:03.670
that's growing down on the bank right here.

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:09.210
We could throw a few more little sticks here and there.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:12.600
Sometimes you can scratch through the paint,

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:14.650
let the white show through and give yourself

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:17.760
a very nice effect of little sticks,

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.760
little trunks happening here in the bushes.

00:26:20.690 --> 00:26:22.850
Or sometimes you might want to take a little bit of paint

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:25.690
and lay in there, just like so.

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:31.670
There we go.

00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:36.060
And let some of these sticks run down on the bank, too,

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:37.020
on the soil area.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:40.820
It's not unusual, very common, in fact,

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.440
to have a lot of little twigs and sticks

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:43.380
growing around the water.

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:48.130
Just let these little things happen.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:54.120
It really produces a tremendous feeling of depth in a pitcher.

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:58.900
Well, you know, we've sat here and answered questions,

00:26:59.000 --> 00:26:59.910
and son of a gun,

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.100
if we hadn't finished a picture.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:06.050
And I think with that I'm gonna sort of

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:07.010
bring this to a close.

00:27:07.620 --> 00:27:10.120
I hope we've answered the majority of your questions

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:11.460
that really bother you.

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:13.740
If you still have problems,

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:15.950
or if there's anything you'd like to know

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:17.130
about this method of painting,

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:18.960
feel free to drop us a line.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.900
We'll be glad to answer any question that you have,

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:23.200
as long as it's not too personal.

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:25.670
We'll just about answer anything.

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:29.980
And on behalf of my son, Steve,

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:34.150
the entire crew here, I would like to thank you

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:37.220
very, very much for being with us through this

00:27:37.660 --> 00:27:40.680
13-part series, and hopefully we'll see you again

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:41.480
in the near future.

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:43.690
And with that, I'm gonna sign this picture

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:47.170
and tell each and every one of you,

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:49.830
thank you from the bottom of my heart.

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:51.510
We've enjoyed being with you.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:54.520
Happy painting.
